implementationPlan.description:
ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, the final step. Load the adversarial-self-review skill. Read your own diff (`git diff -- packages/moe-proxy/src/daemonIdentity.ts packages/moe-proxy/src/daemonIdentity.test.ts`) as an attacker would:
- Twice or concurrently: connect() probes again on every reconnect. Each call owns its own done/timer/req closure, so two probes share nothing.
- Late and hung lookups: a lookup that settles after finish() must open no socket (the done guard), and a hung OS resolver must still end at timeoutMs (the outer timer). A hung dns.promises.lookup keeps a libuv thread busy until the OS gives up. That is accepted, the same as the daemon's healthProbeAddress.
- Inputs:
  - an empty or garbage MOE_DAEMON_HOST still lands in the outer catch or in .catch, giving unknown;
  - IP spellings that WHATWG URL canonicalizes ('127.1', '0x7f000001', percent-encoded) stay on the literal path;
  - a name that resolves to an address where nothing listens gives unknown, which it already gave before, so this is no regression.
- Accepted residuals, to state in the completion summary:
  - a name with several addresses is probed at the first one, while ws may connect to another (Happy Eyeballs);
  - a link-local IPv6 answer with a zone id gets 403, so unknown.
- Scope:
  - only the two files changed;
  - every existing test row is byte-identical (only the vitest and url import lines above them changed);
  - no added line contains a project.json globalRails.forbiddenPatterns string;
  - comments say why, not what.
If this review changes any byte, re-run step 3's command and use that fresh run as the complete_task evidence.
Then refresh the Serena memory decision-daemon-health-host-rule. .serena/ is gitignored, so this is not a tracked write. Its 'Clients and the Host they send' section still says the proxy reads a 403 as 'unknown' and that MOE_DAEMON_HOST must be an IP literal. Replace that with: since task-86d66093 the proxy resolves any MOE_DAEMON_HOST that is not an IP literal or localhost once and dials the IP literal; the launchers' named-host pre-flight probes still send the name, which is a follow-up after WT-19.
